[- Quickstart — five minutes to a running pipeline. -]
[page id='quickstart'
  [meta
    title='Quickstart'
    layout='tutorial'
    nav-order='3'
    audience='evaluator'
    summary='Five minutes from cx installed to your first parse, emit, and evaluator run.'
  ]

  [section id='setup' title='Setup'
    [prose This page assumes cx is on your PATH already; if not, the Install page covers each platform. The rest of Quickstart is a five-minute hands-on session.]
    [code lang='shell' src="cx --version          # confirms the install
mkdir cx-tour && cd cx-tour"]
  ]

  [section id='write' title='Write a Document'
    [prose Save the following as menu.cx. Read it top to bottom — name, attributes, body items, nested children, mixed content. No commas required between attrs; no closing tag to repeat.]
    [code lang='cx' src="[shop name='New Haven Pizza' open=true
  [hours mon-fri=11-22 sat=12-23 sun=closed]
  [menu
    [pizza #margherita size=large crust=thin  price=14.50]
    [pizza #pepperoni  size=large crust=thick price=16.00]
    [side  #salad      price=6.50]]]"]
  ]

  [section id='parse' title='Parse'
    [code lang='shell' src='''cx --ast menu.cx       # JSON view of the parsed tree
cx --cx menu.cx        # canonical CX (attribute order normalised)''']
    [prose The parse never returns a partial tree; a syntax error names the dialect, the byte offset, and the rule that failed.]
  ]

  [section id='emit' title='Emit to Another Format'
    [prose The same tree converts to any of the six dialects CX speaks. Try them.]
    [code lang='shell' src='''cx --json menu.cx
cx --xml  menu.cx
cx --yaml menu.cx
cx --toml menu.cx
cx --md   menu.cx''']
    [prose Each command writes the result to stdout. Round-trip via the binary form (cx --cxdb | cx --from=cxdb) is byte-stable; round-trip via JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown is data-equivalent, with presentation-level differences documented on the comparison page.]
  ]

  [section id='evaluate' title='Evaluate a CXL Template'
    [prose CXL is CX that does things. Save the following as receipt.cxl alongside menu.cx.]
    [code lang='cx' src='[?for p :in //pizza :return [line [?= p/@name] - [?= p/@price] euros;]]']
    [code lang='shell' src='cx eval receipt.cxl --data=menu.cx']
    [prose The result is one receipt line per pizza in the menu. The directive language uses CXPath to query the data; the Tour of 50 Programs walks through the full directive vocabulary.]
  ]

  [section id='hash' title='Compute the Canonical Hash'
    [code lang='shell' src='''cx canonical menu.cx
cx hash menu.cx
cx eq menu.cx some-other-menu.cx''']
    [prose The canonical form strips presentation (comments, attribute order, anchor names, whitespace) and emits a data-equivalent byte-identical form. The hash is SHA-256 over those bytes. Two documents with the same data have the same hash — in any dialect, in any binding.]
  ]

  [section id='from-code' title='Call CX From Your Language'
    [prose Every binding mirrors the CLI. Python by way of example:]
    [code lang='python' src='''import cx
tree = cx.parse(open("menu.cx").read())
print(cx.emit(tree, to="json"))''']
    [prose The Bindings pages cover Python, Go, Rust, V, TypeScript, Java, C-sharp, Ruby, Kotlin, and Swift — each idiomatic, all backed by the same libcx.]
  ]

  [section id='next' title='Where to Next'
    [list
      [item path='tutorial'        label='Tutorial — guided walk-through, atoms to closures, with exercises']
      [item path='tour/data'       label='50 Ways to Love Your CX Data — short examples, simple to complex']
      [item path='tour/programs'   label='50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code — CXL snippets, simple to complex']
      [item path='playground'      label='Playground — try CX in the browser']
      [item path='reference/cli'   label='CLI reference']
    ]
  ]

  [preserve id='author-notes'
    [- Empty by default. -]
  ]
]
